In this lesson we'll look at a neat shortcut called the small angle approximation, which says that for very small angles, sine of x is roughly just x itself. Picture the unit circle. When the angle x, measured in radians, is tiny, the chord and the arc along the circle are almost the same length. That chord is the y coordinate at that point, which is sine of x, so it ends up being nearly equal to x. Now if you sketch the graph of sine of x and the straight line y equals x right near the origin, you'll see they both pass through zero and both have a slope of one there. For really small angles, the two curves sit right on top of each other and look identical. As you move further away from zero, the sine curve gently bends away from the line and starts to fall below it. The numbers make it clear. Sine of zero point one is about zero point zero nine nine eight, almost exactly the same. Sine of zero point five is about zero point four seven nine, already drifting a little lower. And sine of one is about zero point eight four one, where the gap is getting noticeable. So the rule of thumb is simple. Use the approximation when x is small enough that the tiny difference doesn't matter for your problem. Now let's see it in action.